  "statement": "The building directory lists departments one floor at a time, left to right.\n\nGiven the `root` of a binary tree, return its values **level by level**: a list of lists, where the inner list `i` holds the values on depth `i` from left to right. An empty tree gives `[]`.\n\nTrees are passed as a `TreeNode` (with `.val`, `.left`, `.right`; the class is already defined for you). In examples a tree is written in level order with `null` for missing children, for example `[3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]`, and `[]` means an empty tree (`None`).",
  "function": {
    "name": "level_order",
    "params": [
      {
        "name": "root",
        "type": "TreeNode"
      }
    ],
    "returns": "list[list[int]]"
  },
  "constraints": [
    "0 <= number of nodes <= 2000"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [
        [
          3,
          9,
          20,
          null,
          null,
          15,
          7
        ]
      ],
      "expected": [
        [
          3
        ],
        [
          9,
          20
        ],
        [
          15,
          7
        ]
      ],
      "explanation": "Three levels, read left to right."
    },
    {
      "args": [
        [
          1,
          2,
          null,
          4
        ]
      ],
      "expected": [
        [
          1
        ],
        [
          2
        ],
        [
          4
        ]
      ],
      "explanation": "Each level has one node."
    }
  ],
  "starter": {
